{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791658346,"nonce":"g5qPxRSWc3pcDPlxXbC4yTMAGYM=","title":"Lowering makes a global for every occurrence of a string literal","message":"SsaBuilder::create_string_global mints a new private const global for each string literal it lowers, and LoweringContext adds every one to the module. A text that appears N times becomes N globals with the same bytes, each declared and defined as its own Cranelift data object, laid out in JIT data memory, and, where it is boxed (const_boxes), given its own box global and its own box made at start-up.\n\nMeasured on the Mac (release, light build, b1826f91): api.lua's compiled module holds 5541 string-literal globals for 1367 distinct texts; the chunk a one-line load() compiles (`local a, b = ... ; local t = {a = a, b = b}; return t.a + t.b + i`) holds 13 for 7 texts.\n\nFix: LoweringContext keeps the global it made for each text and, when a lowered function brings another global with the same text, points that function's values (and its closures') at the first one instead of adding it. Only globals made by this context's own lowering are merged; prelowered library globals stay as their images have them, since encoded bodies name them by id.","files":null}]}